From be2088a08e2f3d02beadfda5bae90492353a4038 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: SriAkhilSJ <206201543+SriAkhilSJ@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 26 Sep 2026 15:38:44 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(desktop): refresh the manifest receipts with a guarded
 tool, and report every defect at once
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The desktop agent's rebuild left `test_selective_manifest_matches_overlay_files` red on 7 receipts and
correctly refused to hand-edit hash pins — a receipt edited by hand is a rubber stamp. This is the
answer to that: a tool that recomputes receipts reproducibly **and refuses to write while an integrity
problem is present**, so the guard keeps meaning something.

**Root cause of the 7 (checked, not assumed).** `SELECTIVE_MANIFEST.json` was *created* by the same
commit that rewrote those files (`a0dd7787`, the branch base) with receipts computed from an earlier
working copy — the manifest was born stale, there was never a moment when it matched. Two independent
checks say no fork edit was lost: every overlay file still differs from its recorded `upstream_sha256`
(all 5), and the fork markers are present (24 crusorai refs in product.json, 2 in each workbench main,
1 in each build file). The two icons were the real question, and they answer it — their alpha masks
overlap the *verified* brand assets (linux/code.png, server/code-192.png, the 1536 mark) at
**IoU = 1.000**, i.e. the same glyph, correctly sized (150x150, 70x70).

**A second defect was hiding behind the first.** Clearing the hashes let the test reach its next
assertion, which failed: all 9 brand assets cite `generated_from: branding/crusorai-mark.png` while the
documented recipe (docs/DESIGN/FORK_REBRANDING.md) and that assertion name the `.svg`. Checked before
touching it — the standalone PNG is byte-identical to the raster embedded in the SVG
(sha256 2bc21ca0…, 401,307 bytes, both), so this is a provenance citation fix, not a re-render.

So `test_selective_manifest_matches_overlay_files` now collects every problem and reports them
together. One red light per run for a manifest with two problems costs a full diagnose-fix cycle each
time — which is exactly what happened here. Nothing was relaxed: every assertion is still there, and
the failure message names the tool instead of inviting a hand-edit.

`scripts/refresh_selective_manifest.py`:
* `overlay` drift (stale hash) and `generated_from` drift (citation) are refreshed, and reported
  separately because they mean different things;
* **guards, all blocking**: `edit lost` (an overlay byte-identical to its recorded upstream — the
  evidence a fork edit disappeared, and the failure the hand-edit objection was really about),
  `source missing` (a receipt citing an absent source), `upstream pin` (manifest vs UPSTREAM_PIN);
* hashing follows `.gitattributes` (LF-normalized for pinned text, raw for binaries) so a Windows
  checkout with `core.autocrlf=true` produces identical receipts — the pin list is *read* from that
  file, never duplicated;
* writes with `indent=1` + trailing newline, verified byte-identical to the committed formatting, so a
  refresh shows only the receipts that moved: this one is 16 lines (7 hashes + 9 citations).

9 pins in src/tests/test_selective_manifest_tool.py, each verified against the behaviour it replaces:
dropping the `edit lost` guard fails the loss test; dropping citation repair fails the citation test;
perturbing three receipts fails the guard test with all three named. desktop/README.md documents the
tool, so the next drift is not hand-edited either.

21 failed / 262 passed in the wide sweep, down from 22 — every remaining failure is pre-existing in
this sandbox (16 lang-extractors tree-sitter, 3 bridge-transport typing_extensions, and the two bundle
tests that need the rebuilt tracked `out/`).

